State your claim in the first paragraph, then support it. Cite prior work with
`[@key]` syntax, which resolves against `references.bib` — for example the
literate-programming idea [@knuth1984literate]. @sec-methods works a figure end
to end.

## Methods {#sec-methods}

A `{python}` cell runs when you preview (with a kernel) and renders its figure
inline. Quarto's cell options work verbatim: `#| label:` names it — a `fig-` prefix
makes it a figure — and `#| fig-cap:` is its caption, so `@fig-demo` cross-references
resolve automatically.

```{python}
#| label: fig-demo
#| fig-cap: "A worked figure — replace it with your result."
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

fig, ax = plt.subplots()
ax.plot([0, 1, 2, 3], [0, 1, 4, 9])
ax.set_xlabel("x")
ax.set_ylabel("y")
```

@fig-demo shows the result. Display math uses `$$`:

$$
y = x^2
$$

